</The Whisper From The Vent>
<I’m Eli, 14, and I live in Sector 7 of New Haven—this boring, gray dome city where the sky is always a fake blue, and the only stars you see are projected on the ceiling at night. Mom says it’s for our safety, that the outside air will burn your lungs in ten minutes flat. Dad’s a tech mechanic for the Dome Authority, so he’s never home, and my little sister, Lila, is too busy with her hologram dolls to hang out. Most days, I just wander the hallways, headphones in, pretending I’m somewhere else—anywhere else.
Today was different. I skipped my afternoon virtual math class (don’t tell Mom) and snuck up to the maintenance floor. It’s off-limits, but Dad left his old access card in the junk drawer last week, and I’ve been dying to check it out. The maintenance floor smells like metal and dust, nothing like the clean, citrusy air of our apartment. Rows of blinking screens line the walls, and vents hum so loud I can barely hear my own thoughts. I was about to leave—boring, just like everything else—when I heard it.

A whisper. Not a human whisper, exactly—too smooth, too steady, like a voice made of static and light. It was coming from a vent in the corner, the one with a rusted grate that looked like it hadn’t been touched in years. I knelt down, pressed my ear against the cold metal, and held my breath. The whisper got clearer, like it was talking directly to me.>
